Deploying a small Django application behind nginx surprises most people the first time they try it, because three separate moving parts fail in ways that look like the fault of the other two. The application server has to be running and reachable on a local port. The web server has to know that this port exists and has to forward the right headers so that the application can build correct absolute links. And the static assets have to be served by something fast, because asking Python to return a stylesheet is a waste of a worker.

A useful habit is to start from the smallest setup that can possibly work, confirm that a plain page loads, and only then add TLS, caching and compression. People who begin with a fully copied configuration from a blog post usually end up with three unrelated problems at once: a misspelled socket path, a missing trailing slash on a location, and a certificate chain that the browser quietly refuses. Each of these produces a different error page, and none of them mentions the real cause.

The next step is to put the configuration under version control and to keep a comment above every block explaining why the block exists. Six months later nobody remembers which rewrite rule is load-bearing. A short note such as "forward the original scheme so that Django does not downgrade redirects" saves an afternoon. It also makes reviews easier, because a reviewer can see immediately whether a change is meant to alter behaviour or merely to reorganise the file.

Rollbacks deserve the same care. Before changing anything in production, write down the exact command that restores the previous release, and check that the previous release is still available on disk. A rollback that depends on a package registry staying online is not a rollback. Keep the old virtual environment, the old assets and the old configuration file side by side with the new ones, and remove them only after a quiet week.

Finally, test the deployment from outside the machine. Curl from localhost hides proxy problems, and a browser with a warm cache hides header problems. After the site is live, watch the access log for the first hour and confirm that assets are being served by the web server rather than by the application.

The server block that all of this refers to looks like this:

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name shop.example.com;
    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/shop.example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

    location /static/ {
        alias /srv/app/staticfiles/;
        expires 30d;
    }

    location / {
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_pass http://unix:/run/gunicorn.sock;
    }
}

